    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "## Summary\n\nBuilding py-toxcore-c from the current main branch fails to compile against the packaged `libtoxcore-dev` (0.2.18) available in Ubuntu 24.04 (Noble) repositories. The generated Cython C code references typedefs that don't exist in this version of tox.h.\n\n## Environment\n\n- OS: Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS (Noble Numbat)\n- gcc: 13.3.0\n- Python: 3.12.3\n- libtoxcore-dev / libtoxcore2: 0.2.18-1build3 (Ubuntu universe repo)\n- py-toxcore-c: main branch, commit 26bcbaf (\"refactor: Generate Cython bindings from c-toxcore headers via apigen\")\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\napt-get install libtoxcore-dev libtoxcore2 python3-pip\npip install cython setuptools\ngit clone https://github.com/TokTok/py-toxcore-c\ncd py-toxcore-c\ncythonize pytox/toxav/toxav.pyx pytox/toxcore/tox.pyx pytox/toxencryptsave/toxencryptsave.pyx\npython3 setup.py build_ext --inplace\n\n## Actual result\n\npytox/toxav/toxav.c:22458:3: error: unknown type name 'Tox_Friend_Number'\n...\nerror: command '/usr/bin/x86_64-linux-gnu-gcc' failed with exit code 1\n\nAfter defining `Tox_Friend_Number` as `uint32_t` via a compat shim to get past that error, compilation fails again on:\n\npytox/toxav/toxav.c:1690:3: error: unknown type name 'Tox_Options'\n\nChecked directly against the installed header:\n\n$ grep -n \"Tox_Options\" /usr/include/tox/tox.h\n529:struct Tox_Options {\n\nThe installed tox.h declares `struct Tox_Options` without a bare `Tox_Options` typedef alias, and has no standalone `Tox_Friend_Number` typedef at all — friend numbers are still plain `uint32_t` in function signatures. The .pxd files in this repo (`pytox/toxcore/tox.pxd`) assume these typedefs already exist upstream in tox.h:\n\nctypedef uint32_t Tox_Friend_Number\n\n## Expected result\n\nEither the build succeeds against the currently packaged/released libtoxcore, or the README/build docs specify a minimum required c-toxcore version/commit so users know they need to build c-toxcore from source rather than use their distro's package.\n\n## Question\n\nIs main currently expected to only work against a specific unreleased c-toxcore revision (i.e. ahead of the 0.2.18/0.2.20 release line)? If so, could the README note the required c-toxcore commit/tag, or could a version guard be added so the failure is clearer than a raw compiler error? Happy to test against a specific c-toxcore ref if pointed at one.\n\n---\n\nThis is my first time filing an issue like this, so please let me know if anything about the format, level of detail, or reproduction steps isn't quite right — I'll take it into account and do better next time.\n\n",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Hi all — looking for guidance on the current state of Tox client libraries for scripting/automation.\n\nI need to build a small bridge so an external agent can programmatically send/receive Tox messages (read incoming messages, send messages to a friend by ID, react to friend status changes — not a full client, just message I/O).\n\nI tried a few options and hit dead ends:\n\n- py-toxcore-c (TokTok/py-toxcore-c): the .pyx bindings reference symbols like `Tox_Friend_Number` and a typedef'd `Tox_Options` that don't exist in the packaged libtoxcore (0.2.18, Ubuntu/Debian repos) — looks like it's generated against a newer/unreleased c-toxcore header, so it fails to compile out of the box.\n- PyTox (oxij/PyTox): appears unmaintained, last meaningful activity years ago.\n- pytoxcore (abbat/pytoxcore): last commit in 2020, likely stale against current toxcore API too.\n\nBefore I sink more time into patching type mismatches or building c-toxcore from source to match a specific binding commit — is there a library/approach people are actually using today for this kind of automation? Ctypes-based wrapper, a Rust/Go binding with a thin RPC layer, or something else entirely?\n\nRough shape of what I'm trying to build (pseudocode, not attached to any specific API):\n\non_friend_message(friend_id, message):\nlog_incoming(friend_id, message)\nresponse = agent.process(message)\ntox.send_message(friend_id, response)\ndef send_message(friend_id, text):\ntox.friend_send_message(friend_id, text)\n\nAny pointers — even \"avoid X, use Y instead\" — appreciated. Thanks!",
